WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.750 --> 00:00:05.810
You may have seen this chart since the start
of the coronavirus pandemic.

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:09.100
In one image, it appears to capture the state of each nation’s battle

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:12.560
in the global war against the virus.

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:18.100
But like all data visualizations, its design&nbsp;
tends to emphasize some things and hides others.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:22.560
So here are 4 things we need to know to understand
this chart.

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:26.940
First, this is not a chart of all coronavirus
cases.

00:00:26.949 --> 00:00:29.920
It’s only showing us confirmed cases.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:33.590
That means each line doesn’t just reflect
the state of the outbreak in a country, but

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:39.050
also how aggressively that country is testing
people for the virus.

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:42.480
Take a look at Japan and South Korea earlier
in the pandemic.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.950
Japan’s outbreak looked pretty small in
comparison.

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:50.550
But the available data on testing shows us
that South Korea had tested vastly more people

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:54.620
than Japan did, even though their population
is less than half as big.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:01:00.090
And now, as Japan slowly increases their testing,
the outbreak there looks more worrying.

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:03.240
It’s a good reminder that we can’t understand
case data

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:05.720
without some sense of the testing levels.

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:08.220
And that’s especially true for lower-income
countries

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:11.800
where we know their testing capacity is limited.

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:15.000
The second thing to know is that the scale for the y-axis on this chart

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:16.800
is a bit different from most charts.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:20.380
It’s called a logarithmic or log scale.

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:26.380
On a typical linear scale, you divide the
space by adding the same value over and over.

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:30.409
The log scale is made by multiplying a value,
in this case, ten.

00:01:30.409 --> 00:01:36.520
100 times 10 is 1000, times 10 is 10,000,
times 10 is 100,000.

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:39.660
That means that there’s no fixed amount
of space on this chart

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:42.759
for a certain number of cases.

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:45.860
So the first 100,000 cases take up this much
space

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:50.480
and then the next 100,000 cases get just this much.

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:55.420
The higher the numbers, the more visibly squished
they become on a log scale.

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:57.520
So why do it this way?

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:01.100
Well, let’s take the 5 countries with the
largest outbreaks right now, and rewind them

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:03.689
back to March 17th.

00:02:03.689 --> 00:02:06.960
On a linear scale, it looked like things were
pretty bad in Italy

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:08.860
but the others were doing better.

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:11.880
The log scale offered a much clearer warning:

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:16.220
we were all on the same path of exponential growth.

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:20.220
It’s the nature of infectious disease that
numbers get big fast.

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:24.840
So it makes sense for numbers to get big fast
on the chart too.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:29.080
Fast forward a few weeks and the linear scale shows cases climbing and climbing

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:32.700
while the log chart shows curves that are flattening.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:36.610
As governments have implemented lockdowns
and social distancing, the virus is spreading

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:41.540
at a slower rate than before, which isn’t
very visible on the linear scale.

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:45.540
But keep in mind that the difference between
this dot and this dot

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:48.820
is more than 32,000 people.

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:52.850
And the log chart tends to downplay just how
many more confirmed cases there are in the

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:55.840
US than in the other countries.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:58.180
Which brings us to the third thing to know

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:02.000
about our chart: it doesn’t account for
population size.

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:06.361
When you adjust for population, really small
countries like Iceland and Luxembourg&nbsp; appear

00:03:06.361 --> 00:03:11.420
to have the biggest outbreaks for their size,
which may reflect higher testing rates.

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:16.489
The US and China have much bigger populations
so their curves drop a bit.

00:03:16.489 --> 00:03:20.489
But the size of a country doesn’t really
affect the growth rate of its cases, and it

00:03:20.489 --> 00:03:23.720
doesn’t tell us much about how much the
country is struggling.

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:27.830
It just pushes smaller countries up on the
chart and tends to hide the fact that the

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:31.620
outbreak is especially bad in certain regions
of bigger countries,

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.360
like the state of New York.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:39.660
And the last thing to know about our chart
is that the x-axis doesn’t plot time by

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:43.120
the date,&nbsp;
but by the number of days since the country

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.240
recorded 100 confirmed cases.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:47.280
For Italy, that was February 24.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:49.620
For Turkey, March 19.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:53.120
When they’re all layered on top of each
other, it allows us to compare the trajectory

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:56.600
of the outbreaks,
but it tends to obscure the fact that the

00:03:56.600 --> 00:04:00.480
pandemic hit some countries before it hit
others.

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:07.600
The world watched as tens of thousands of
cases appeared in China.

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:15.760
Then big outbreaks in South Korea, Italy,
and Iran, sent a message about what was to come.

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:23.200
Two weeks after South Korea reported its 100th
case, the United States’ did the same.

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:28.100
In a situation where actions taken early can
have a much bigger impact than actions taken

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:34.740
later, time is a crucial factor. and we have
to remember that some governments had more

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:36.160
time than others.

